       <h2>Step 1: Oil it thinly</h2>
       <p>With the pan still warm, rub a teaspoon of oil over every surface, including the handle and the bottom. Then wipe it all off again as if you had made a mistake. What stays behind is enough; a thick coat turns sticky.</p>
-      <img src="/images/skillet-oiled.png" width="64" height="48">
+      <img src="/images/skillet-oiled.png" alt="Cast iron skillet rubbed with a thin, even coat of oil" width="64" height="48">
 
       <h2>Step 2: Bake it upside down</h2>
       <p>Put the skillet upside down on the middle rack, with a sheet of foil on the rack below to catch drips. Bake for one hour at 450°F (230°C), then turn the oven off and let the pan cool inside it.</p>
-      <img src="/images/skillet-oven.png" width="64" height="48">
+      <img src="/images/skillet-oven.png" alt="Cast iron skillet baking upside down on the oven's middle rack" width="64" height="48">
 
       <h2>Step 3: Repeat</h2>
       <p>I did three rounds. After the third, the surface was an even, dull black, and a fried egg slid around with a little butter. Your pan may need more or fewer rounds.</p>
